;; manifest/dependency-substitution.edn — every external dependency the
;; kotoba-lang org still has, and what it is supposed to become.
;;
;; ## Why this file exists
;;
;; CLAUDE.md fixes the runtime order as `kotoba wasm` > `clojurewasm` >
;; ClojureScript > nbb, demotes JVM and babashka to last resort, and
;; `90-docs/migration/kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover.edn` declares
;; `:new-jvm-runtime-dependencies :forbidden`. Neither says what the CURRENT
;; external surface is, so "is this dependency allowed?" had no answer that
;; could be looked up -- only re-derived, differently each time.
;;
;; Measured 2026-08-26 across 2,129 of 2,143 west-registered kotoba-lang
;; paths, the whole external surface is 26 distinct JVM runtime coordinates
;; and 110 npm runtime packages. That is small enough to decide once, per
;; coordinate, and then check.
;;
;; ## The finding that reshaped the work
;;
;; Most of it is already replaceable. The foundational stdlib
;; (ADR-kotoba-lang-foundational-stdlib: coll/spec/json/wit/async/time/fs/
;; http/io/test/fmt/lsp + scheduler/store/lint, plus net/crypto/tls/kv) and
;; the origin-plane spec mirrors (org-nist-sha2, org-ietf-blake2,
;; org-ietf-ed25519, org-ietf-cbor, io-multiformats, io-ipld-car,
;; org-ietf-deflate, org-ietf-x509, org-ietf-asn1, org-postgresql-wire) cover
;; the large majority by usage count. The work those entries name is REWIRING
;; an import, not writing a library.
;;
;; So `:disposition` is the useful axis, not the coordinate:
;;
;;   :structural           the host language, its compiler, or a render
;;                         backend. Not a library anyone here can write; the
;;                         exit is the .kotoba migration, tracked elsewhere.
;;   :first-party-exists   a first-party replacement is shipped and the work
;;                         is to switch the import.
;;   :gap-closed           there was no first-party equivalent and one was
;;                         written. Names the repository.
;;   :gap-open             there is no first-party equivalent and none has
;;                         been written. Names what it would be called and
;;                         why it has not been done.
;;   :frozen-legacy        covered by an existing cutover contract with its
;;                         own exit evidence. Do not re-litigate here.
;;   :tooling              dev-time only; never resolved by shipped code.
;;
;; ## Two rules this file is the source of truth for
;;
;; 1. A coordinate that appears in a kotoba-lang checkout and NOT in this
;;    file is a newly acquired external dependency. The cutover contract
;;    forbids that for JVM runtime deps; for the others it is at minimum an
;;    undiscussed one. `scripts/verify-dependency-substitution.cljs` reports
;;    it.
;; 2. `:target` is a claim that can be checked. It must name a repository
;;    that exists, and the detector fails if it does not -- a substitution
;;    ledger pointing at a library nobody wrote is worse than no ledger,
;;    because it reads as though the work were already possible.
;;
;; ## What this file deliberately does not contain
;;
;; Usage counts. They were measured to decide the dispositions and they are
;; in the ADR, but a count written here becomes a constant that gets quoted
;; after it stops being true -- the failure mode CLAUDE.md documents about
;; its own fleet-ci section. Re-measure with
;; `scripts/verify-dependency-substitution.cljs --edn`.

{:dependency-substitution/version 1
 :dependency-substitution/adr "adr-2608260200-kotoba-lang-external-dependency-substitution"
 :dependency-substitution/measured "2026-08-26"
 :dependency-substitution/scope "orgs/kotoba-lang"

 :dependency-substitution/coverage-2026-09-09
