  ;; ------------------------------------------------ first-party exists: rewire
  {:coordinate "org.clojure/data.json" :ecosystem :maven :disposition :first-party-exists
   :target "kotoba-lang/json" :kind :implementation :note "Layer 1 of the foundational stdlib. encode/decode, deterministic key order."
   :qualification
   "The note names `deterministic key order` as a property of the target. It is
    also a DIFFERENCE from the source, and that is the half a rewire has to
    plan for. Measured 2026-09-10.

      (array-map \"zebra\" 1 \"apple\" 2 \"mango\" 3)
      json.core/encode           -> {\"apple\":2,\"mango\":3,\"zebra\":1}
      clojure.data.json/write-str -> {\"zebra\":1,\"apple\":2,\"mango\":3}

    `json.data-json` is API-complete for the surface these 27 repos use --
    `read-str` / `write-str` / `read`, trailing kwargs or an options map,
    `:key-fn` applied recursively -- and its docstring is right that the swap
    is `a require-line change rather than hundreds of call-site edits`. That is
    a statement about arities. **It is not a statement about bytes.**

    No round-trip test can see the difference: decode(encode(x)) = x under
    both. So the usual evidence for a codec swap does not discriminate here.

    Where the bytes are load-bearing, measured rather than assumed:

    - 29 files across 9 of the 27 repos hold a JSON write and a digest in the
      same file. That is a list of places to look, not a list of defects.
    - Two of the strongest-looking candidates are NOT data paths:
      `network-awai/cloud-itonami` `net_kotobase.clj` and
      `cloud-itonami-isic-5820` `crm/kotobase.clj` both hash a NAME STRING
      (`kotobase/db/<did>/<db>`) to build a graph CID. Co-location of a JSON
      writer and a digest in one file proves nothing.
    - One confirmed JSON-to-digest path:
      `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app` `.../app/acme.clj`, the RFC 7638 JWK
      thumbprint (`b64u(sha256(utf8(write-str (jwk key))))`). **There the swap
      is safe and strengthening.** RFC 7638 requires lexicographic member
      order, and today that is held by a hand-written `array-map` whose own
      docstring says `in the order RFC 7638 requires ... and it is not an
      accident here`. Sorting makes structural what is currently remembered.
      Nothing tests that array-map's order; under a sorting encoder nothing
      needs to.
    - The JWS uses in the same file are not in this class: `protected` and
      `payload` are transmitted as the exact bytes that were signed, so member
      order cannot invalidate them.
    - 7 of the 27 repos assert on JSON string literals in tests. Those go RED
      at swap time. That is the good failure -- visible, at the moment of the
      change, in the repo making it.

    The hazard that remains is the one nothing here covers: a receiver
    comparing bytes produced elsewhere in insertion order. Nothing in these 27
    was found doing that, and `not found` is weaker than `absent`.

    One thing makes the rewire cheaper than the file count suggests: several of
    these repos already INJECT the writer (`:json-write clojure.data.json/write-str`
    passed in as a parameter), so the swap is one line at the injection site --
    and, for the same reason, the byte change reaches every consumer of that
    seam at once. Change it where it is injected, not where it is called.

    First rewire landed 2026-09-10 (cloud-itonami/za-actor, the whole repo's only
    JVM runtime dep). Two things it taught, both cheaper to read here than to
    rediscover:

    **A repo with `:local/root \"../../kotoba-lang/...\"` cannot be built from a
    worktree outside the superproject** -- `clojure` fails resolving the
    classpath before it reaches your change. 8 of the 9 remaining clean repos
    are in this class. Give the worktree the same relative shape
    (`<tmp>/orgs/<org>/<repo>`) and symlink `<tmp>/orgs/kotoba-lang` at the real
    one.

    **Check that the suite loaded the file you changed.** za-actor's
    `clojure -M:test` returned 42 tests / 86 assertions / 0 failures and never
    required `za-actor.deploy`, the only file the commit touched. Forcing the
    namespace to load fails -- on unmodified main too: it requires
    `kotoba.net.jvm-host` and the repo declares nothing providing it. So that
    coordinate was being carried for a namespace that does not load, and the
    green was evidence of nothing. Per-namespace
    `(require (quote <ns>))` after the edit, and `git stash` to tell a
    pre-existing failure from your own.

    Order of work: land the 20 repos with no digest and no JSON-literal test
    first, let the 7 literal-test repos go red and fix the fixtures, and treat
    the 9 digest-adjacent repos as a read-before-swap list of 29 files."}
