  {:coordinate "org.clojure/tools.reader" :ecosystem :maven :disposition :first-party-exists
   :target "kotoba-lang/reader" :kind :implementation :note "kotoba-lang/edn covers the bounded-EDN half."
   :qualification
   "TRUE for `.kotoba` source, and that is how the substitution has actually
    landed: amu's production classpath went from one tools.reader jar to zero
    on 2026-09-08 once kotoba-sema read with one reader on both hosts.

    NOT a drop-in for `kotoba/src/kotoba/runtime.clj`, which is the last
    declaration of this coordinate in orgs/kotoba-lang. Measured 2026-09-10.
    That call site reads `.cljc` for a CHOSEN HOST:

      (reader/read {:read-cond :allow :features #{reader-target} :eof ::eof} rdr)

    and `kotoba-lang/reader` declines host-feature selection on purpose -- its
    README says reader conditionals select `:kotoba` or `:default`, NEVER a
    host feature, so a `.kotoba` source cannot read differently depending on
    where it is read. Probed directly against the target:

      #?(:kotoba 1 :default 2)  ->  1
      #?(:clj    1 :default 2)  ->  2      (not the :clj branch)
      #?(:cljs   1 :default 2)  ->  2

    The two readers are doing different jobs, and the target's refusal is a
    property it was built to have. Swapping it in here would silently read the
    `:default` branch of every `#?(:clj ...)` in a `.cljc` file.

    One half of the swap IS available and is strictly better: `runtime.clj`
    binds `*read-eval* false` so a crafted source cannot execute JVM code at
    read time. `kotoba-lang/reader` has no eval at all -- `#=(+ 1 2)` answers
    `unsupported reader dispatch` -- so the property is structural there rather
    than a binding someone can forget.

    The exit for this call site is therefore a decision about `runtime.clj`,
    not a library swap: either it stops reading `.cljc` for a chosen host, or
    the target grows host features and loses the property it was built for.
    Not decided here."}
