   :note "Split by use: certificates to org-ietf-x509, DER to org-ietf-asn1, signatures to org-ietf-ed25519 or eth-crypto. Not a single swap."
   :qualification
   "The note above is already careful -- it splits by use and says `Not a
    single swap`. What it does not cover is that the three uses it lists are
    not the three uses that exist. Measured 2026-09-10 from what each consumer
    actually imports: none of them parses a certificate, none decodes bare
    DER, and none signs with Ed25519. `:target org-ietf-x509` is the first
    thing a reader or a wave bot sees, and it fits no call site.

    kagi -- `Argon2BytesGenerator` + `Argon2Parameters`. A password KDF, not
    X.509 at anything. The right target EXISTS: `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-argon2`,
    RFC 9106 Argon2d/i/id in portable `.cljc`, whose own README says it was
    written because pqh's `IKdf` seam had `a real gap -- there was no
    in-language implementation to serve as the reference a provider is checked
    against`. kagi already carries the seam to plug it into: `defprotocol
    Provider`, its own `暗号プリミティブの差し替え境界`.

    Per ADR-2608301100's ladder this is a REFERENCE-PROVIDER move, not a
    removal: Argon2 is a deliberately slow KDF and a pure `.cljc` one may be
    far slower again, the way ChaCha seal measured 3.25 ms against @noble's
    0.0065 ms. Reference first, production hot path measured before it moves.

    pqh -- `pqc.crypto.mlkem`, `pqc.crypto.mldsa`, digests, generators.
    Post-quantum primitives. Searched before concluding absence: no
    first-party ML-KEM or ML-DSA exists (`kotoba-lang/kyber` is
    `com-etzhayyim-app-kyber`, a business app). Genuine `:host-boundary`.

    murakumo -- `cert.jcajce`, `openssl.jcajce`, `operator.jcajce`,
    `jce.provider`, `asn1.x509`, `asn1.x500`. Certificate ISSUANCE and PEM
    writing. Parsing is not issuing. Genuine `:host-boundary` until something
    first-party issues.

    One floor applies to all three and is worth writing down where the
    substitution is proposed rather than where it is attempted: a green test
    suite is not sufficient evidence for replacing a cryptographic primitive.
    A wrong swap here is a security defect that compiles."}
  {:coordinate "org.bouncycastle/bcpkix-jdk18on" :ecosystem :maven :disposition :first-party-exists
   :target "kotoba-lang/org-ietf-cms" :kind :implementation
   :qualification
   "One consumer, one file, one function -- and the target does a different
    job. Measured 2026-09-10.

    murakumo `src/murakumo/overlay/cert.clj` is the whole surface. All three
    bcpkix classes sit in `issue-self-signed!` and `pem-string`:
    `JcaX509v3CertificateBuilder` + `JcaX509CertificateConverter` assemble a
    90-day self-signed X.509 for local QUIC, `JcaContentSignerBuilder`
    (`SHA256withRSA`) signs it, `JcaPEMWriter` writes it out. This is
    certificate ISSUANCE. `org-ietf-cms` is RFC 5652 SignedData -- parse,
    verify, and `build-signed-data`, a signed MESSAGE container. Nothing here
    signs a CMS message, so the target fits no call site, the same defect the
    bcprov entry has with `org-ietf-x509`.

    Both bouncycastle coordinates land in this one file: bcprov supplies its
    `asn1.x500` / `asn1.x509` / `jce.provider` half. They are one substitution,
    not two.

    A first-party issuer DOES exist, and this ledger names neither half of it.
    `kotoba-lang/io-storj-node` carries, in portable `.cljc` with no
    bouncycastle:

      storj.node.certificate  -- `ca-tbs`, `leaf-tbs`, `certificate`,
                                 `key-usage`, serials, extensions. TBS
                                 assembly and signature wrapping.
      storj.node.pem          -- RFC 7468 encode/decode with its own base64,
                                 checked against RFC 4648's vectors and
                                 against a PEM file Go wrote.

    The two are not equally reusable, and the distinction is the point.

    `storj.node.pem` is general. Its docstring says why it was written there:
    `Nothing in this workspace's byte libraries has it` -- io-multiformats
    carries base58 and base32 because multihash and CID need those. It
    replaces `JcaPEMWriter` directly.

    `storj.node.certificate` is deliberately Storj-shaped and says so:
    subject and issuer are both `O=Storj` on CA and leaf, validity is Go's
    zero time twice (year 1 -- Storj node certificates do not expire, so the
    namespace needs no clock), and the identity version rides in extension
    `2.999.2.1` as a raw byte. murakumo needs a 90-day cert with subjectAltName
    for a host or IP. So this is NOT a drop-in. What it is, is proof that
    building a certificate is expressible in portable `.cljc` here -- which is
    a different and weaker claim than `a target exists`, and the ledger should
    say the weaker one.

    Signing and key generation stay a host boundary in both. storj reaches
    them through `IKeyMaterial` (`(p/-sign key-material private :ecdsa-sha256
    tbs)`); murakumo reaches them through JCA `SHA256withRSA`. A seam, not a
    gap.

    The same floor as bcprov applies, for the same reason: a green test suite
    is not sufficient evidence for replacing a cryptographic primitive. A
    certificate that parses is not a certificate that is correct -- QUIC will
    present whatever it is handed.

    Method note. A grep for `TBSCertificate` and `tbs-certificate` across
    kotoba-lang returned zero, and `storj.node.certificate` names its functions
    `ca-tbs` and `leaf-tbs`. Concluding absence from that grep would have been
    wrong; the builder surfaced only because `storj.node.mint`'s docstring
    describes what it calls. Searching for a name one guessed is not
    searching."}
