  {:coordinate "@noble/post-quantum" :ecosystem :npm :disposition :host-boundary
   :note "CORRECTED 2026-09-06. The entry said :first-party-exists with :target kotoba-lang/kyber, and that target is not an ML-KEM implementation -- it is com-etzhayyim-app-kyber, a LangGraph business portal with 33 files and zero crypto among them. The name collided with Kyber the KEM and nobody looked. This is the failure rule 2 above describes, one level deeper than the detector reaches: the detector checks that the target repository EXISTS, and it does, so the ledger read as though a first-party ML-KEM were already available. Three namespaces bind this package -- kotoba-lang/envelope's envelope.kem, kotoba-lang/pqh's pq_noble.cljs and kotoba-lang/kagi's kagi.crypto.noble -- and all three are bindings, not implementations. FIPS 203 is delicate lattice cryptography where a subtle error is silent, and unlike ed25519 there is no in-tree oracle to differential against; kotoba-lang/security's conformance/crypto/vectors are three BouncyCastle-generated vectors, enough to QUALIFY a provider and nowhere near enough to develop one. The answer taken instead is qualification: envelope.qualify pins the module digest and runs those vectors through the deployed module, and kotoba.security.crypto-policy/evaluate-pq-provider judges the result. Reopen with a real budget and an oracle, not by renaming a target."}
  {:coordinate "@noble/curves/bls12-381" :ecosystem :npm :disposition :host-boundary
